1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,200
Almost every banana shipped
between countries for sale

2
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:06,200
is the same variety, the Cavendish.

3
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:09,600
And here's the strange part:
every Cavendish plant

4
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:10,920
is a clone.

5
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:14,080
They're all grown from
pieces of earlier plants,

6
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:17,360
so they all carry
essentially the same DNA.

7
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:20,720
That's useful, because
every banana comes out just

8
00:00:20,760 --> 00:00:22,480
the way growers want.

9
00:00:22,520 --> 00:00:25,280
But a fungal disease called TR4 is now

10
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:28,160
spreading through banana
plantations in more than twenty

11
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:29,440
countries.

12
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:31,840
Why would sharing the same DNA make that

13
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:33,040
a serious problem?

14
00:00:34,267 --> 00:00:35,946
Start with the big idea.

15
00:00:36,507 --> 00:00:39,946
Every individual plant
or animal eventually dies.

16
00:00:40,427 --> 00:00:44,506
A species only continues if
individuals reproduce, making

17
00:00:44,587 --> 00:00:47,946
offspring, and pass on
their DNA, the inherited

18
00:00:48,027 --> 00:00:50,906
information that shapes
how each offspring grows.

19
00:00:51,547 --> 00:00:52,986
And that's not quite enough.

20
00:00:53,467 --> 00:00:56,026
Those offspring also have
to survive long enough

21
00:00:56,107 --> 00:00:57,546
to reproduce themselves.

22
00:00:58,107 --> 00:01:01,706
If that chain keeps going,
generation after generation,

23
00:01:01,747 --> 00:01:03,386
the species continues.

24
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:06,520
There are two ways to make offspring.

25
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:09,720
The first is asexual reproduction.

26
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:12,840
One parent makes offspring
on its own, without

27
00:01:12,920 --> 00:01:14,960
any reproductive cells joining.

28
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:17,320
A strawberry plant sends out a runner that

29
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:18,920
grows into a new plant.

30
00:01:19,160 --> 00:01:21,080
A bacterium splits in two.

31
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:23,600
A yeast cell grows a bud that breaks

32
00:01:23,640 --> 00:01:24,360
away.

33
00:01:24,680 --> 00:01:26,920
In each case the offspring gets a copy

34
00:01:26,960 --> 00:01:29,720
of the parent's DNA, so it's a clone.

35
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:32,800
It's fast, it needs no partner, and a

36
00:01:32,840 --> 00:01:35,560
successful plant can fill a field quickly.

37
00:01:35,880 --> 00:01:38,040
Copying isn't perfect, though.

38
00:01:38,160 --> 00:01:42,440
Occasional copying errors,
called mutations, mean even clones

39
00:01:42,520 --> 00:01:43,560
can differ a little.

40
00:01:44,767 --> 00:01:47,606
The second way is sexual reproduction.

41
00:01:47,887 --> 00:01:51,406
Here, two reproductive
cells called gametes, like a

42
00:01:51,487 --> 00:01:54,126
sperm cell and an egg cell, join together.

43
00:01:54,607 --> 00:01:56,686
To see why that matters, we need one

44
00:01:56,767 --> 00:01:57,486
more idea.

45
00:01:57,967 --> 00:02:01,166
Inside each cell, DNA
is packed into structures

46
00:02:01,247 --> 00:02:02,526
called chromosomes.

47
00:02:03,007 --> 00:02:05,886
In humans and many other
organisms, most body

48
00:02:05,967 --> 00:02:08,126
cells carry two sets of chromosomes.

49
00:02:08,607 --> 00:02:10,606
A gamete carries just one set.

50
00:02:11,167 --> 00:02:13,966
So when two gametes join, one set plus

51
00:02:14,047 --> 00:02:16,366
one set gives the new offspring a full

52
00:02:16,527 --> 00:02:19,246
two sets, usually one from each parent.

53
00:02:20,467 --> 00:02:22,786
A gene is a section of DNA that

54
00:02:22,867 --> 00:02:26,066
carries the instructions for
one characteristic, like the

55
00:02:26,147 --> 00:02:27,426
size of a strawberry.

56
00:02:27,747 --> 00:02:31,346
Genes come in different
versions, called alleles: one

57
00:02:31,427 --> 00:02:34,666
version might give bigger
fruit, another smaller fruit.

58
00:02:34,947 --> 00:02:36,266
Here's the key part.

59
00:02:36,547 --> 00:02:39,186
The gametes made by one parent aren't all

60
00:02:39,387 --> 00:02:40,186
identical.

61
00:02:40,467 --> 00:02:42,626
Each one carries a different mix of that

62
00:02:42,707 --> 00:02:43,906
parent's alleles.

63
00:02:44,147 --> 00:02:46,386
And which sperm meets which egg comes down

64
00:02:46,467 --> 00:02:47,186
to chance.

65
00:02:47,747 --> 00:02:49,906
So every seedling ends up with its own

66
00:02:49,987 --> 00:02:51,346
combination of alleles.

67
00:02:51,827 --> 00:02:54,146
That's why seedlings from
the same two strawberry

68
00:02:54,227 --> 00:02:56,106
parents all look a bit different.

69
00:02:56,387 --> 00:03:00,066
Those differences between
individuals are called variation.

70
00:03:00,547 --> 00:03:03,026
Each seedling isn't
a blend of its parents,

71
00:03:03,067 --> 00:03:04,546
and it isn't a copy.

72
00:03:04,587 --> 00:03:06,066
It's a new mix.

73
00:03:06,147 --> 00:03:09,186
The cost is that sexual
reproduction is slower,

74
00:03:09,347 --> 00:03:11,826
takes more energy,
and usually needs a mate.

75
00:03:13,034 --> 00:03:14,473
So which is better?

76
00:03:14,714 --> 00:03:16,313
It depends on the conditions.

77
00:03:16,554 --> 00:03:19,273
When conditions are stable,
copying a plant that

78
00:03:19,514 --> 00:03:22,313
already does well is a
great strategy, because

79
00:03:22,394 --> 00:03:24,313
every copy does well too.

80
00:03:24,394 --> 00:03:27,113
But when conditions change,
say a new disease

81
00:03:27,234 --> 00:03:29,593
arrives, it's a different story.

82
00:03:29,714 --> 00:03:31,993
Whether a plant can
resist a disease depends

83
00:03:32,154 --> 00:03:33,993
partly on its alleles.

84
00:03:34,034 --> 00:03:36,153
In a group of clones, they all carry

85
00:03:36,234 --> 00:03:38,873
the same alleles,
so either they all resist

86
00:03:38,954 --> 00:03:40,953
it, or none of them do.

87
00:03:41,034 --> 00:03:43,593
In a varied group, some plants may carry

88
00:03:43,634 --> 00:03:45,993
an allele that helps
them survive, and those

89
00:03:46,074 --> 00:03:48,473
survivors keep the species going.

90
00:03:48,594 --> 00:03:50,393
That's the Cavendish problem.

91
00:03:50,554 --> 00:03:53,193
Every plant has the same DNA, so once

92
00:03:53,274 --> 00:03:55,833
TR4 can attack one, it can attack them

93
00:03:55,914 --> 00:03:56,553
all.

94
00:03:56,874 --> 00:03:59,513
Here's a way to remember it: asexual is

95
00:03:59,594 --> 00:04:03,433
fast and alike, sexual is slow and varied.

96
00:04:03,994 --> 00:04:05,913
Alike suits stable conditions.

97
00:04:06,394 --> 00:04:08,633
Varied helps when conditions change.

98
00:04:09,834 --> 00:04:11,993
Here's how you'd turn this into a strong

99
00:04:12,194 --> 00:04:14,233
answer if a question asks you to compare

100
00:04:14,314 --> 00:04:16,953
sexual and asexual reproduction.

101
00:04:17,274 --> 00:04:21,193
A comparison needs both similarities
and differences, organised

102
00:04:21,274 --> 00:04:22,153
by criteria.

103
00:04:22,794 --> 00:04:26,233
Start with variation:
clones have almost the same

104
00:04:26,314 --> 00:04:29,513
DNA, while sexual offspring
get new combinations of

105
00:04:29,634 --> 00:04:30,313
alleles.

106
00:04:30,554 --> 00:04:34,633
Then speed: copying builds
a population quickly, joining

107
00:04:34,754 --> 00:04:36,313
gametes is slower.

108
00:04:36,474 --> 00:04:39,593
Then partners and energy:
no mate is needed

109
00:04:39,674 --> 00:04:43,353
for asexual reproduction, while
sexual reproduction usually needs

110
00:04:43,434 --> 00:04:45,513
one and costs more energy.

111
00:04:45,674 --> 00:04:48,633
And one similarity: both pass DNA to the

112
00:04:48,714 --> 00:04:51,193
next generation,
and both can be changed by

113
00:04:51,514 --> 00:04:52,393
mutation.

114
00:04:52,594 --> 00:04:54,873
Then each row becomes
a sentence, like this

115
00:04:54,954 --> 00:04:55,593
one.

116
00:04:55,714 --> 00:05:00,313
Asexual reproduction produces
offspring with almost identical DNA,

117
00:05:00,434 --> 00:05:04,193
whereas sexual reproduction produces
new combinations of alleles.

118
00:05:04,474 --> 00:05:08,073
Finish by linking to conditions:
stable conditions favour

119
00:05:08,234 --> 00:05:11,353
asexual, changing
conditions favour sexual.

120
00:05:12,567 --> 00:05:13,206
Your turn.

121
00:05:13,767 --> 00:05:17,526
For each situation, decide
whether asexual or sexual

122
00:05:17,607 --> 00:05:20,806
reproduction would better
help the species continue, and

123
00:05:21,047 --> 00:05:22,126
explain why.

124
00:05:22,567 --> 00:05:25,366
One: bacteria living in a warm pond where

125
00:05:25,407 --> 00:05:27,766
the conditions have
stayed the same for years.

126
00:05:28,407 --> 00:05:30,726
Two: a single plant that has reached an

127
00:05:30,807 --> 00:05:32,886
island with no others of its kind, and

128
00:05:32,967 --> 00:05:34,806
it can't fertilise itself.

129
00:05:35,127 --> 00:05:37,606
Three: a strawberry
plant that can send out

130
00:05:37,727 --> 00:05:40,406
runners and also make seeds, in a field

131
00:05:40,487 --> 00:05:43,446
that's getting hotter
and drier every year.

132
00:05:43,527 --> 00:05:45,766
Which method gives
its offspring the better chance

133
00:05:45,807 --> 00:05:47,046
in the long run?

134
00:05:47,167 --> 00:05:49,126
Pause the video if you'd like more time.

135
00:06:14,534 --> 00:06:18,213
In the stable pond, asexual
reproduction works well.

136
00:06:18,454 --> 00:06:21,653
The bacteria already
suit their pond, so fast

137
00:06:21,734 --> 00:06:24,693
copying builds the population
and keeps that winning

138
00:06:24,774 --> 00:06:25,493
combination.

139
00:06:26,134 --> 00:06:29,573
If you chose sexual because
variation sounds better,

140
00:06:29,774 --> 00:06:33,093
remember variation only
helps when conditions change.

141
00:06:33,734 --> 00:06:36,853
On the island, asexual
reproduction is the plant's

142
00:06:36,934 --> 00:06:37,733
only option.

143
00:06:38,214 --> 00:06:40,133
With no mate and no way to fertilise

144
00:06:40,214 --> 00:06:42,933
itself, it can only start a population by

145
00:06:43,014 --> 00:06:46,293
copying itself, for
example by growing runners.

146
00:06:46,614 --> 00:06:48,853
And for the strawberry
in the warming field,

147
00:06:49,054 --> 00:06:52,453
sexual reproduction gives
the better long-term chance.

148
00:06:52,574 --> 00:06:55,813
Its seeds produce varied
seedlings, so some may

149
00:06:55,934 --> 00:06:57,893
carry alleles that cope
better with heat and

150
00:06:57,934 --> 00:06:58,933
drought.

151
00:06:59,014 --> 00:07:00,933
Runners would only make more copies of a

152
00:07:01,014 --> 00:07:03,893
plant that may struggle as
conditions keep changing.

153
00:07:05,100 --> 00:07:08,540
So, a species continues when DNA is passed

154
00:07:08,620 --> 00:07:11,580
on and offspring survive to reproduce.

155
00:07:11,900 --> 00:07:14,860
Asexual reproduction is fast and alike.

156
00:07:15,340 --> 00:07:18,140
Sexual reproduction is slow and varied.

157
00:07:18,620 --> 00:07:21,180
Which one helps more depends
on whether conditions

158
00:07:21,260 --> 00:07:23,420
stay the same or change.

159
00:07:23,900 --> 00:07:26,380
Next, we'll see how different
animals actually bring

160
00:07:26,460 --> 00:07:27,820
sperm and egg together.

161
00:07:29,034 --> 00:07:30,473
Here are the key notes.

162
00:07:30,714 --> 00:07:32,633
Pause the video if you need more time

163
00:07:32,714 --> 00:07:33,513
to copy them.
